    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe collision-resolution logic lives in a sensible unit (it's cohesive with \"generate a unique customer code\"), but the change is written in a style that clashes with the surrounding file's conventions.\n\n**Fully-qualified names instead of imports (idiomatic \u2014 the main issue).** The new code inlines `java.util.Set`, `java.util.Objects`, and `java.util.stream.Collectors`:\n- `java.util.Set` and `java.util.Objects` are *already imported* at the top of this file (lines 36 and 35). Fully-qualifying them here is redundant and directly inconsistent with the rest of the class, which uses the short names.\n- `Collectors` / `Collectors.toSet()` should get a normal `import java.util.stream.Collectors;` (and the file has no `java.util.stream.*` import yet) to match the established import block, rather than an inline FQN.\n\nA Spring maintainer reading this method would find the inline `java.util.` prefixes jarring against a file that otherwise imports everything cleanly. Rewrite using the short names (`Set`, `Objects`, `Collectors`) and add the one missing import.\n\nMinor, optional: the loading of all owners' codes into a `Set` for uniqueness is a data-access concern sitting inside the code-derivation helper; if a natural spot exists (e.g. a repository query for existing codes), pushing the lookup toward the repository layer would keep `generateCustomerCode` focused on derivation. Not blocking on its own \u2014 the import style is the concrete fix.",
